    "result_text": "Both test classes ran and the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe business-day rule now extends to public holidays. The logic was cleanly localized to a single collaborator, `BusinessDays`, so every downstream consumer of the adjusted registration date (daily limit, membership number, bulk-signup warning) picks up the change for free.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`BusinessDays.java`** \u2014 Added the fixed public-holiday calendar (`2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28`) as an immutable `Set<LocalDate>`. Replaced the two special-case weekend branches with a loop that advances one day at a time while the date is not a business day, and introduced a private `isBusinessDay` predicate that owns the single definition of \"business day\" (not a weekend, not a holiday). This correctly handles chains \u2014 e.g. Fri `2026-12-25` (holiday) \u2192 weekend \u2192 Mon `2026-12-28` (holiday) \u2192 Tue `2026-12-29`.\n\n2. **`RollRegistrationDateToBusinessDay.java`** \u2014 Updated the javadoc to describe the holiday roll-forward (behaviour unchanged; it still just delegates to `BusinessDays.rollForward`).\n\n3. **`BusinessDaysTest.java`** (new) \u2014 A focused unit test in the same package (to reach the package-private class) covering plain weekdays, weekend rolls, single weekday holidays, a holiday landing on a weekend, and consecutive holidays.\n\n**Tests:** Full suite green \u2014 `Tests run: 8, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` (7 new unit tests + the existing acceptance invariant test, which still confirms the default registration date is never a weekend).",
